  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis this afternoon depict a \nmid/upper wave ejecting out of the Rockies into the Plains. An \nanomalous fetch of moisture characterized by PWs ranging from 150-\n200% of normal for this time of year accompanies this feature and \nspans from Baja California to the Central Plains and beyond. \nAdditionally, a region of showers and weak storms is seen across the \nTX/OK Panhandles and southwest KS amidst strong 850-700 mb moisture \ntransport. These will continue shifting northeast and increase in \ncoverage across the forecast area later this afternoon and evening \nas the wave continues its northeast track. Similar to Thursday, the \noverall severe threat appears low at this time considering \npseudoadiabatic lapse rates, though effective shear around \n25-35 kts could result in some storm organization leading to \nstrong to marginally severe wind gusts mainly west of I-135. \nHeavy rainfall may also lead to localized flooding concerns due \nto the persistent tropical airmass and moist antecedent soil \nconditions.\n\nActivity will continue into Saturday morning across eastern KS as \nthe mid/upper impulse shifts toward the Upper Mississippi Valley. By \nlate afternoon, warming surface temperatures within the moist \nairmass will yield MLCAPE in the 1000-2000 J/kg range across \nportions of central and south central KS ahead of an approaching \nweak surface trough. This may lead to isolated strong to severe \nstorms capable of strong winds and marginally severe hail through \nthe evening hours, thought effective shear around 20-25 kts should \npreclude widespread severe weather. As has been the case for the \nlast several days, the primary hazard will remain heavy rainfall \nleading to localized flooding.\n\nRain chances will push eastward heading into Sunday morning with \nperhaps a handful of showers lingering across southeast KS by \nsunrise. Besides this, most of the area should remain dry Sunday \ninto Monday with mid/upper ridging overhead. This break in rain \nchances will be short-lived, however, as global models carry a \npotent wave into the southwest CONUS by late Monday night. \nStrengthening low-level moisture transport over the region should \npromote the development of showers and storms by Tuesday as the \nupper low slides eastward, and by the middle of the week, this upper \nsystem looks to absorb a tropical disturbance coming out of Baja \nCalifornia. This will allow for continued showers and storms through \nThursday within another anomalously-moist airmass. This solution \nwill likely be dependent on how progressive the upper wave is, \nhowever, and with little run-to-run consensus among long-range \ndeterministic solutions, confidence in the details (such as rainfall \ntotals and duration) remains low at this time. Stay tuned.",
